  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 944 PM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe closed low over the Great Basin yesterday has weakened into an\nopen trough extending across Utah into southern Nevada. This\nsystem will move across northern Arizona tonight into tomorrow\nmorning, then across New Mexico through the day tomorrow\n(Saturday). \n\nA few spotty showers will graze the northern Gila Region in the \nmorning, and some sprinkles could reach northern Grant County, but\nmostly we'll just see some uptick in cloud cover and breezy west-\nsouthwest winds. By afternoon, gusts 35 to 40 mph will be common,\nwith a few gusts around 45 mph possible along east-facing mountain\nslopes and exposed higher elevation ridgelines. No dust is\nexpected.\n\nHigh pressure will build in Saturday night, then the race will be\non to see if winds can diminish fast enough to maximize\nradiational cooling. Dewpoints dropping into the upper-10s to\nlower-20s and a lack of cloud cover will help. Weakest gradient\nwill be north of the I-10 corridor in New Mexico. Upper-10s in the\nGila cold spots are likely. Jornada Range and the Hatch Valley\nlook like a fair bet for reaching the freezing mark. The Mesilla\nValley is a 50/50 toss-up. We'll get another chance Sunday\nnight/Monday morning as winds will be lighter. \n\nAfter notching a high 10 degrees above normal at ELP today\n(Friday, and 75 degrees), temperatures will drop Saturday and\nSunday with highs Sunday topping out near 60 over much of the\nlowlands, about 3 to 5 degrees below normal.\n\nAnother weak trough will sweep through on Monday, with little wind\nand very low precip chances. Models still having a tough time with\nthe pattern across the CONUS late week, but benign weather is most\nfavored.",
